Task: You are creating a game with different character classes. Implement the `Warrior` and `Mage` classes to inherit from `Character` and override their `attack` method to describe their unique combat actions.

Submitted code:
```
class Character {
  constructor(name) {
    this.name = name;
  }

  attack() {
    return `${this.name} performs a generic attack.`;
  }
}

class Warrior extends Character {
  constructor(name, weapon) {
    // 1. Initialize the parent name property
    super(name);
    this.weapon = weapon;
  }

  attack() {
    // 2. Override with Warrior-specific logic
    return `${this.name} slashes with their ${this.weapon}!`;
  }
}

class Mage extends Character {
  constructor(name, spell) {
    // 1. Initialize the parent name property
    super(name);
    this.spell = spell;
  }

  attack() {
    // 2. Override with Mage-specific logic
    return `${this.name} casts ${this.spell} for magical damage!`;
  }
}

// --- Example Usage ---
const hero = new Warrior("Arthas", "Frostmourne");
const wizard = new Mage("Jaina", "Blizzard");

console.log(hero.attack());   // Arthas slashes with their Frostmourne!
console.log(wizard.attack()); // Jaina casts Blizzard for magical damage!

```

Does this code correctly implement the polymorphism concept? Check for proper OOP usage.
